.TH FOXTROT 7 "2026" "foxtrot 0.0.1" "Foxtrot Library Reference"
.SH NAME
foxtrot \- an I/O-agnostic C library for SSH-2 and SFTP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <foxtrot/session.h>
.sp
cc \-o prog prog.c \(ga\&pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs foxtrot\(ga
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
foxtrot is a library for building SSH clients and servers.
It speaks SSH-2 (RFC 4251 to 4254 and the extensions in current use) and SFTP
version 3, with the user authentication methods, key types, certificates and
file formats that OpenSSH uses.
It is secure by design: what is known to be weak is not there to be enabled.
.PP
It does no input or output.
It does not open a socket, read a file, look at a clock or start a thread.
The application hands it bytes that arrived, takes the bytes it wants sent,
and tells it the time.
That is the whole interface to the world, and it fits any event loop, any
transport (a socket, a pipe, a test), and any file system.
.SH THE MODEL
.IP \(bu 3
Bytes in, bytes out.
.BR gf_session_receive (3)
takes whatever arrived, in any chunking, down to one byte at a time;
the parsers are incremental and keep what they need.
Output goes to the
.I write
callback, which is required and may be called from inside any call.
.IP \(bu 3
Time is an argument.
Calls take the current time in microseconds on any clock that does not go
backwards.
.B gf_session_timeout
says when the library next needs to be woken, and
.B gf_session_on_timeout
is what to call then.
Certificates and revocation lists use the wall clock in seconds, supplied the
same way.
.IP \(bu 3
Events are callbacks, set in a structure of function pointers and a
.I user
pointer that is passed back.
What a callback is given (a key, a name, the data of a message) is valid only
for the call unless the header says otherwise; copy what you keep.
A callback that returns nonzero ends the session.
.IP \(bu 3
One session is used from one thread at a time.
Separate sessions are independent.
Tables that are only read after they are loaded (known hosts, authorized keys,
revocation lists, trust stores) may be shared by readers.
.IP \(bu 3
Nothing in the library writes to standard error, exits or aborts on bad
input from a peer.
It will end a session that misbehaves, and say why.
.SH LAYERS
Each header has a page,
.BI foxtrot_ name (3).
The layers can be used on their own; the session is the usual way in.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_session (3)
The transport, user authentication and channels joined in one object with one
set of events, and the routing, deadlines and enforcement they need between
them.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_conn (3), " foxtrot_kex" (3), " foxtrot_packet" (3), " foxtrot_ident" (3), " foxtrot_kexinit" (3)
The transport: identification, binary packets, algorithm negotiation, key
exchange (including the post-quantum hybrids), and rekeying.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_auth (3), " foxtrot_authkeys" (3)
User authentication as client and server (public key, password,
keyboard-interactive, certificates, security keys, external signers), and
.I authorized_keys
files.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_chan (3)
The connection protocol: channels with strict flow control, session requests,
TCP and Unix socket forwarding.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_sftp (3)
SFTP version 3, client and server, with OpenSSH's extensions.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_key (3), " foxtrot_keyfmt" (3)
Keys, OpenSSH certificates (reading and making them), security keys,
OpenSSH private key files (also under a passphrase), and other formats: PEM,
PKCS#8, RFC 4716, PuTTY.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_knownhosts (3), " foxtrot_krl" (3), " foxtrot_x509" (3)
Host key checking, key revocation lists, and X.509 chains as keys (RFC 6187,
with GnuTLS).
.TP
.BR foxtrot_agent (3)
The messages of the ssh-agent protocol, for a client whose keys are in an
agent: framing, the list of identities, signing requests and replies, and
session-bind.  The socket is the application's.
.TP
.BR foxtrot_policy (3), " foxtrot_crypto" (3), " foxtrot_wire" (3), " foxtrot_status" (3)
The algorithm policy, the cryptographic primitives (all from libgcrypt), the
wire encoding, and the status codes.
.SH ERRORS
Functions that can fail return a status:
.B GF_OK
(0) or a negative
.B GF_ERR_*
value, described in
.BR foxtrot_status (3).
.B gf_strerror
turns one into text.
Some parsers return
.B GF_NEED_MORE
(1) for input that ended in the middle of something, which is not an error.
.PP
Functions that give a result return it through a pointer argument.
Objects are made by
.B gf_*_new
and freed by
.BR gf_*_free ,
which wipes secrets; keys that a configuration points to must outlive the
object that uses it, as the headers say.
.SH A CLIENT, IN OUTLINE
.nf
gf_session_config cfg = { 0 };
gf_session_events ev = { 0 };
gf_session *s;

cfg.client_auth.user = "alice";
cfg.client_auth.keys = keys;            /* with private parts */
cfg.client_auth.nkeys = 1;
ev.user = &my_state;
ev.write = my_write;                    /* send these bytes */
ev.host_key = my_host_key;              /* trust this key?  0 for yes */
ev.authenticated = my_ready;            /* open a channel here */
gf_session_client_new (&s, &cfg, &ev);
gf_session_start (s, now_us ());
/* then: whatever arrives goes to gf_session_receive (s, now_us (),
   data, len); and when gf_session_timeout (s) passes,
   gf_session_on_timeout (s, now_us ()). */
.fi
.PP
A client with no
.I host_key
callback trusts nothing, so that forgetting it is not a vulnerability.
The programs in the examples directory (see below) are complete and short.
.SH SECURITY POLICY
Absent by design, not disabled by default:
SSH protocol 1; CBC and CTR ciphers and separate MACs in the transport (the
ciphers are all AEAD); SHA-1 signatures and
.IR ssh-rsa ;
DSA; ECDSA over P-521; finite-field Diffie-Hellman; compression; agent and X11
forwarding (a client can have its keys in an agent, see
.BR foxtrot_agent (3)); host-based authentication; and the old password change.
Keys are held to a minimum strength, and the Terrapin attack is closed by
strict key exchange, which is required of the peer.
.PP
SHA-1 appears in three places only, to recognise names and keys and never to
protect them: the hashed names of known_hosts, OCSP responder names, and the
hashes in revocation lists.
.PP
The library needs libgcrypt 1.12.4 or later (earlier versions can be made to
abort the process by a peer's Ed25519 key), checked when it is built and when
it is first used.
GnuTLS is optional, for X.509 keys.
The complete statement is the chapter
.I Security policy
of the manual.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/foxtrot/examples/
Example programs, in source:
.B ssh-run
(run a command on a host),
.B sftp-get
(fetch a file),
.B ssh-serve
(a small server using authorized_keys),
.B keyinfo
(what is in a key file, in any format),
.B krl-check
and
.BR cert-sign .
.B example.h
is the part of an application that the library leaves out: sockets, a clock and
an event loop.
